    "lawCode": "Amd §470, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5300\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to research\ntobacco products\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nExempts medical research tobacco products from tax and regulatory\nrequirements intended for commercial tobacco products.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one exempts medical research tobacco products from the defi-\nnitions of \"cigarette\" and \"tobacco product\" in the Tax Law and defines\n\"research tobacco products.\"\n\nSection two states the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nResearch tobacco is tobacco used only for medical research purposes and\nshipped to medical researchers from out of state. A recent proposal by\nthe Department of Tax and Finance would subject research tobacco to\nsimilar taxation requirements as commercial tobacco. This would effec-\ntively cut off supply to New York's research institutions.\n\nThe Kentucky Tobacco Research and Development Center (KTRDC) supplies\ntobacco to NY institutions to study chronic obstructive pulmonary disor-\nder. KTRDC's product is sent directly to New York research institutions\nwithout risk of diversion or sale on the commercial market, nor does\nthis research involve human consumption of tobacco. For these reasons,\nthe Department has historically exempted KTRDC from cigarette tax stamp-\ning requirements under statutory and regulatory authority (Tax Law\nsection 471-A \"Use tax on cigarettes;\" and 20 NYCRR section 76.1 \"Ciga-\nrette tax\" / \"Exemption from tax.\")\n\nIn 2018, however, the Department informed research institutions that\nunder a new interpretation of the law and regulations, KTRDC's products\nwould no longer be exempt from stamping requirements and that KTRDC\nwould have to become a certified manufacturer under the Master Settle-\nment Agreement. This change in longstanding interpretation did not\nfollow any statutory or regulatory change.\n\nKTRDC is unable to become a certified manufacturer in New York without\njeopardizing its position nationally. The company sells to dozens of\nstates who have never treated it as a commercial tobacco company, nor is\nit subject to such requirements in its home state. Rather than risk\naccepting a precedent for regulators nationally, KTRDC may simply quit\nthe New York market - and medical researchers have been unable to iden-\ntify a comparable supplier. Even if an alternative was found, changing\nsuppliers mid-study would throw the validity of current research into\ndoubt.\n\nThe Department's longtime exemption of research tobacco from certain\nregulatory requirements has facilitated medical research without any\ndiversion or other problems. This reinterpretation would effectively\nblock future research at New York institutions, and potentially invali-\ndate current studies. In response, this legislation defines \"research\ntobacco\" so to limit it clearly to tobacco held by certain institutions,\nfor purposes of tobacco-related health research, and that shall not be\noffered to consumers; and carves out such \"research tobacco\" from the\ndefinition of \"cigarettes\" upon which taxes are imposed.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: New bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State, as research tobacco has not been previously taxed.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate\n",
